      {
        "id": "def-rack-fundamental-quandle-and-adjoint-group",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Racks, diagram fundamental quandles, and adjoint groups",
        "deps": [
          "def-quandle-and-knot-coloring",
          "def-regular-oriented-link-diagram",
          "def-group-presentation"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "proof_strategy": "not-applicable",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "V. O. Manturov, Knot Theory, Chapter 5, §§5.1–5.2, printed pp. 49–55",
              "url": "https://varf.ru/rudn/manturov/book.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "J. Scott Carter, A Survey of Quandle Ideas, §§2.1 and 2.2, arXiv:1002.4429v2, pp. 3–8",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1002.4429"
            },
            {
              "title": "Sam Nelson, Link invariants from finite racks, §§2.1–2.4, arXiv:0808.0029v3, pp. 2–6",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0808.0029"
            }
          ]
        },
        "statement": "A **rack** is a set $R$ with a binary operation $*:R\\times R\\to R$ such\nthat for every $y\\in R$ the right translation $R_y(x)=x*y$ is a bijection\nand $(x*y)*z=(x*z)*(y*z)$ for all $x,y,z\\in R$. A **quandle** is a rack\nsatisfying $x*x=x$ for every $x$. For either structure, write\n$x\\mathbin{\\bar *}y=R_y^{-1}(x)$.\n\nFor a regular oriented link diagram $D$, let $A(D)$ be its finite set of\nmaximal under-arcs. Form the set of finite terms recursively: each symbol\n$x_a$ for $a\\in A(D)$ is a term, and if $s,t$ are terms then $s*t$ and\n$s\\mathbin{\\bar *}t$ are terms. Let $\\sim$ be the intersection of all\nequivalence relations on these terms that are compatible with both operations\nand contain the quandle identities: idempotency, right invertibility, and\nright self-distributivity, together with the crossing relations.\nAt a crossing with source and target under-arcs $u,v$ and overarc $o$, the\ncrossing relation is $x_v=x_u*x_o$; reversing source and target gives the\nequivalent relation with $\\bar *$. The quotient term set $Q(D)$ is the\n**diagram quandle**, or the diagram presentation of the **fundamental\nquandle**. Its independence of diagram is proved in the Theorem.\n\nFor any quandle $Q$, its **adjoint group** $\\operatorname{Adj}(Q)$ is the\ngroup presentation with generators $e_q$ for all $q\\in Q$ and relators\n$e_{q*r}=e_r^{-1}e_qe_r$ for all $q,r\\in Q$. A group presentation\n$\\langle X\\mid S\\rangle$ means the quotient of the free group on $X$ by the\nnormal closure of the relator words $S$.\n\nThe diagram presentation and the adjoint group are algebraic constructions;\nthis definition makes no completeness or knot-classification assertion."
      },
