        },
        "statement": "Let $R$ be a rack, with right translations $R_y(x)=x*y$. The kink map\n$\\pi:R\\to R$, $\\pi(x)=x*x$, is a bijection. Its inverse is\n$\\pi^{-1}(z)=R_z^{-1}(z)$."
      },
      {
        "id": "alg-finite-rack-quandle-coloring-from-table",
        "kind": "algorithm",
        "title": "Check a finite rack table and enumerate diagram colorings",
        "deps": [
          "def-rack-fundamental-quandle-and-adjoint-group",
          "def-regular-oriented-link-diagram"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "proof_strategy": "not-applicable",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Sam Nelson, Link invariants from finite racks, §§2.1, 2.2 and 3, arXiv:0808.0029v3, pp. 2–7",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0808.0029"
            },
            {
              "title": "J. Scott Carter, A Survey of Quandle Ideas, §2, arXiv:1002.4429v2, pp. 3–8",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1002.4429"
            }
          ]
        },
        "statement": "Input an integer $m\\ge1$, a table $T\\in\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}^{m\\times m}$ with\nentry $T[x,y]$ intended to be $x*y$, a mode \"rack\" or \"quandle\", a finite\nregular oriented link diagram $D$ with $a$ arcs and $c$ crossings, and fixed\nfinite orderings of its arcs and crossings. If the table is not $m\\times m$,\nreturn invalid-table-shape with its row lengths. If an entry is outside\n$\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$, return invalid-table-entry with its row, column, and value.\nOtherwise perform these checks in order:\n\n1. For each $y=1,\\ldots,m$, check whether the column\n   $(T[1,y],\\ldots,T[m,y])$ is a permutation of $\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$. If not,\n   return right-translation-not-bijective with $y$, a repeated value and a\n   missing value.\n2. For each ordered triple $(x,y,z)$ in lexicographic order, check\n   $T[T[x,y],z]=T[T[x,z],T[y,z]]$. If not, return\n   self-distributivity-fails with $(x,y,z)$ and both table values.\n3. In quandle mode, check $T[x,x]=x$ for each $x$ in increasing order. If\n   not, return idempotency-fails with $x$ and $T[x,x]$.\n\nIf the selected axioms pass, enumerate the $m^a$ functions from the supplied\nordered arcs of $D$ to $\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$ in lexicographic order. Retain an assignment\nexactly when it satisfies the crossing rule in the rack/quandle Definition,\nusing the inverse column permutation when the rule calls for the right\ndivision. Return the retained assignments and their number. No correctness or\ncomplexity claim is part of this algorithm's Definition."
      },
